ture fell by the  wayside largely because they could rely  on federal regulations to prevent them  from getting involved it wasn't until  the 1980s that Fox came along and you  know for whatever you feel about that  network or Fox News or anything else the  history is that there had been three  networks throughout the past it looked  like that was going to be the the fate  going forward and it was only because  you had an upstart challenging the  federal regulations that existed at the  time that you were able to create than a  fourth Network and then of course cable  came along well cable was already in  development but cable networks were  coming along as well creating much more  choice and diversity largely because  those networks were free of the kinds of  universal content regulations that  existed for broadcasting so even where  you can start by diagnosing a given  problem and thinking that the solution  to that is that we need someone to go in  and lay down  our history is that when someone does  you have almost exactly the opposite  result of what you would hope would come  about if I can follow what that that  that in some cases is the result of  often a maturing industry working with  government in some cases doing exactly  what they what they can do to create  barriers to entry from upstarts is there  a concern and and the government can in  to do this this is just what TV did this  with movies kind of intimidating an  industry into adopting some sort of  quote-unquote voluntary standards I'm  thinking of the movie rating systems  which was developed by Jack Valenti who  was a Johnson administration a part of  the Johnson administration everybody  went along with it the government the  the print the studio's the exhibitors  because they wanted at the movies to be  a a place where families could go to a  friendly place but at some say that it  has really inhibited independent  filmmakers could there be a point where  the government approaches or begins to  pressure Google which owns YouTube you  know let's let's have some kind of  standard here we won't impose it but we  will strongly suggest you create it is  this is this pattern likely to repeat  itself or is it just to endemic right  now with with YouTube and other video  sources that you really can't can't get  that level of granularity or ratings or  or standards and practices into into the  internet especially entertainment I look  forward to hearing Bob starts I would  say that's already happened at the back  end of the sites that you have kind of  coercion where where these companies  basically have to go along to give up  their information of their customers so  maybe at the front end I'm not so sure  but the back end of the site and all the  information is being collected you know  I don't think the courts have clear I  think they there was an early the course  of clearly said this is you can't  transmit isn't  they've been working closely to do just  that well just to start with the issue  of what kind of information companies  are giving over the issue isn't whether  or not the companies can or should give  information under the right  circumstances the issue has always been  what process is required for that just  as you know the government can search  your home if they have a search warrant  they can wiretap your phone if they have  a search warrant it is the process that  protects our constitutional liberties  and when you're dealing with third-party  intermediaries the companies that  provide those services then the question  is what kind of process is required  since 1967 a key Supreme Court decision  in that time it did require a search  warrant to have a wiretap law was then  codified in 1968 and 1986 that applied  to that as well  I would think after you know after 9/11  when the Patriot Act was passed cobbled  together  oddly enough from proposals I had  testified against during the Clinton  administration the Patriot Act loosened  those standards and made it more  possible for information to be shared  and so again we get back to the question  and sistas the same debate over what  kind of process is required before  telecommunications companies and network  managers are required to share that  information because short of that kind  of requirement they're not in the  business for giving up your personal  information but the question that you  started with which goes to government  and private companies collaborating I  would take issue with the example of the  movie rating system in part because MPAA  is a client and but but and I'm just  saying that in the name so for full  disclosure purposes but also because you  have to consider the alternative if you  go back to the early days of film and  the Hays office that was adopted by the  film production code that was a much  heavier type of censorship than  existed you had all of these pre-code  Hollywood films that are classics that  nobody had really heard about since  because things clamped down and there it  was sort of very strongly influenced by  government and it was it was a time  before courts even recognized First  Amendment protections for films and so  the industry stepped up to sort of  forestall government action and I think  all would acknowledge that it went way  too far but things began to change in  the 1960s and not only had courts begun  to strike down these various local state  censorship laws but Hollywood was also  trying to change with the changing legal  climate and also the cultural changes  that were happening in the 60s and so it  was then that the MPAA rating system the  production that you mentioned Jack  Valenti was instrumental in helping to  create did create a system so that  basically it's so that people can be  informed going into a film what they  might be likely to expect is it perfect  well no rating system is perfect but as  a private editorial judgment for what  kind of film to expect it's not a bad  guideline they routinely get about 80 to  85 percent consumer approval rate  ratings for whether or not people think  that the ratings are useful the question  of whether or not ratings can be used by  government is something very different  though any time you involve government  in either implementing these or  enforcing ratings decisions then  disasters occur and as a matter of fact  a lot of local governments when the  rating system was devised in the 60s  began to think this is such a fine idea  we should make it compulsory  matter of fact there was one case  involving a sheriff in North Carolina  that thought the rating system was such  a dandy idea that he was going to  prohibit any drive-in theater in his  jurisdiction from showing anything above  AG rating so fortunately the courts have  always made short work of those kinds of  ratings obviously taking care let's  drive through to  they have exactly they have they have  always been struck down and I think  anytime you combine sort of an official  improviser with a private editorial  system a rating system then you're going  to have a constitutional issue we we are  about out of time I want to thank our  guests Robert Korn Revere First  Amendment attorney Pete Tucker of the  fight back dot-org and Pete's may not  agree with everybody in this room but if  you visit his website you can see what's  being done with real street-level  journalism on the website and it is a  great example of how the Internet can be  used to do reporting that is not  reported in the mainstream media so  thanks for your attendance thank for you  online thank you to our guests thank you
 

